      "id": "lem-pe26-discrete-trace-schur-equivalence",
      "concern": "The uniform geometric hypothesis needs adjudication. Step 1.1 asserts: 'The finite chart count and chart constants are uniform under [F1].' The model explicitly supplies bounded side count, lower corner-angle bounds, inradius comparable to diameter and 'a uniform interior-cone parameter', but does not define a uniform boundary graph/neighborhood radius. A pointwise interior-cone condition alone does not justify this assertion. Consider Ω_ε = ((−1,1)×(0,2)) minus the closed notch [−ε/2,ε/2]×[1,2]. These eight-sided polygons have uniformly bounded diameter, comparable inradius, right/reentrant-right angles and pointwise interior cones of fixed aperture and height. On aligned quasiuniform meshes define the P1 function to be zero below y=1, −φ(y) on the left arm and +φ(y) on the right arm, where φ rises linearly from 0 to 1 on [1,5/4] and is 1 above 5/4. Its energy for ρ=1 is 4(2−ε) ≤ 8, and it satisfies zero Dirichlet data on the bottom side. Its trace differs by 2 on the facing notch segments of length L=3/4. Just one ordered cross-boundary contribution to the stated Euclidean-distance seminorm is 8[(L/ε) arctan(L/ε) − (1/2)log(1+(L/ε)²)], which diverges as ε⁻¹. Harmonic minimality therefore contradicts a uniform positive lower trace-Schur constant under those explicit bounds. Unless 'uniform interior-cone parameter' is intended to impose a stronger uniform local boundary-chart condition excluding this family, the uniform claim is false. The same geometry also challenges the uniform cross-edge-integral assertion in lem-pe26-scaled-averaging-energy-bound, step 1.1; its downstream condition-number proof needs disposition. The consulted source retains domain-dependent trace constants: [Rheinbach, Theorem 1.1.1, printed p. 13](https://d-nb.info/983219192/34).",
